Rhythm

Rhythm is a weekly planner for building healthy habits around a busy schedule. You add your fixed commitments (classes, work shifts, other responsibilities), plan workouts, meals, and habits into the open time, check items off as you finish them, and log anything you did that you didn't plan.

Live app: https://rhythm-garrettbeck1.replit.app/login

What it does
Accounts: sign up with email and password, log in, and log out. Each user sees only their own schedule.
Plan your week: add events in six categories: Workout, Meal, Habit, Class, Work, and Responsibility.
Check off planned items when you complete them.
Log unplanned events, like a pickup game or a dinner out, so your week shows what really happened.
Edit and delete any event. Deleting asks "Are you sure?" first.
Star important events and see them on the Starred page.
Search, filter, and sort events by category, planned or unplanned, done or not done, title, notes, and date.
Dashboard: a Chart.js chart of planned versus completed items per week (this week plus the 3 before it), plus this week's completion rate, workouts completed, and unplanned events logged.
Weekly calendar view: a Monday–Sunday grid with fixed commitments in gray and healthy items in color. This is the home page after login.
Recurring routines: set something once (e.g., "Tue 6am rower intervals") and it appears every week.
Conflict warning: you get a warning if you plan a workout, meal, or habit on top of a class, work, or responsibility block.
How to use it
Sign up for an account.
Add your classes and work shifts for the week.
Add Routines for things you do every week, like a training plan.
Plan meals, workouts, and habits into your open time.
Check items off as you finish them, and use Log unplanned for anything extra.
Open the Dashboard to see how closely your week matched your plan.

Main pages: /week (calendar), /events (list with search, filters, and sort), /starred, /routines, /dashboard.

Tools used
Tool	What it's for
Replit Agent	AI coding agent that built the features from my prompts (see prompts/)
Node.js + Express	The web server and all pages
PostgreSQL (Replit's built-in database)	Stores users, events, categories, routines, and stars
Drizzle ORM	Defines the tables (db/schema.js) and runs database queries
Email/password auth built into the app	Passwords are hashed with scrypt (Node's built-in crypto); logins use a signed, HTTP-only session cookie (auth.js)
Chart.js	The dashboard chart
Node's built-in test runner (node --test)	Automated tests
Replit Deployments	Hosts the live app
GitHub	Version control. Every change goes through an Issue, a branch, and a pull request.
Database

The main tables are users, categories, events (the main item), routines, and stars (a junction table linking users to the events they starred). There's also a routine_skips table that the agent added for recurring routines. See schema.md for every column and relationship.

How to run it
On Replit (easiest)
Open the project on Replit, or import this repo at replit.com/import.
Make sure a PostgreSQL database is attached and a SESSION_SECRET secret (at least 32 characters) is set in Tools → Secrets.
Click Run. The app starts on port 5000.
Locally

You need Node.js and a PostgreSQL database.

bash
git clone https://github.com/garrettbeck-sudo/Rhythm.git
cd Rhythm
npm install

Set these environment variables:

Variable	What it is
DATABASE_URL	Your PostgreSQL connection string
SESSION_SECRET	Any random string of at least 32 characters, used to sign login cookies
PORT	Optional. Defaults to 5000

Then create the tables, add the 6 categories, and start the app:

bash
npm run db:push
npm run db:seed
npm start

Open http://localhost:5000.

Never commit secrets or a .env file to GitHub.

Running the tests
bash
npm test

The data-isolation and auth tests use the database, so DATABASE_URL and SESSION_SECRET must be set.

The two required tests:

Data isolation (test/data-isolation.test.js): creates User A with a "6 mi tempo run" on Thursday, logs in as User B, and checks that User B sees no events.
Dashboard (dashboard/metrics.test.js): with 4 planned events this week, 3 completed, plus 1 unplanned event, the completion rate is 75%. Unplanned events don't count toward the rate.

There are also tests for the calendar layout, routine generation, event handling, and page rendering.

